  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Consider a circle with centre $C$. A point on the circumference of the circle is labelled $P$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q4a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "A point is chosen uniformly on the circumference of the circle. Find the probability that the point is closer to the point $P$ than $C$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$",
          "worked": "Let the circle have radius $r$. The perpendicular bisector of $CP$ separates points closer to $P$ from points closer to $C$. It meets the circumference at $A$ and $B$. Since its distance from $C$ is $\\frac{r}{2}$, each boundary radius makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with $CP$. The favourable arc therefore subtends $120^\\circ$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(E)&=\\frac{120^\\circ}{360^\\circ}\\\\\\Pr(E)&=\\frac13.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q4b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "A point is chosen uniformly in the interior of the circle. Find the probability that the point is closer to the point $P$ than $C$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p40-q4b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\dfrac{4\\pi-3\\sqrt3}{12\\pi}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p40-q4b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Subtract the triangle from the 120° sector, then divide by the circle’s area.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let the circle have radius $r$. The favourable region is the circular segment on the $P$ side of the perpendicular bisector of $CP$. Its boundary chord subtends $120^\\circ$ at $C$, as in part (a). Subtract the triangle formed by the two boundary radii from the sector.\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{sector}}&=\\frac{120}{360}\\pi r^2\\\\A_{\\text{sector}}&=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{3}\\\\A_{\\text{triangle}}&=\\frac12r^2\\sin120^\\circ\\\\A_{\\text{triangle}}&=\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{4}\\\\A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{4}\\\\A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac{4\\pi-3\\sqrt3}{12}r^2.\\end{align*}$$\nUniform selection makes probability proportional to area.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(E)&=\\frac{A_{\\text{segment}}}{\\pi r^2}\\\\\\Pr(E)&=\\frac{4\\pi-3\\sqrt3}{12\\pi}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
  },
